--TEST--
`Core\Task::map` answers an `array<U>` whose `U` is the callback's own declared return type, so nothing at the use site is `mixed` — `rule:concurrency/map-preserves-keys-and-order`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2's signature is `map(array<T>, callable, {limit?, deadline?}): array<U>`,
// and `U` binds from the one callback the whole call shares — which is the
// contrast that makes `all` and `map` two members rather than one. `all` needs
// a per-field binding site because an anonymous object carries a different callable
// in every field; `map` writes its callback once, so an ordinary
// `CoreTy::CallableTo` already says everything there is to say.
//
// The scheduler half is not on disk (`crates/nvs-stdlib/src/task.rs` owns what
// the body is until it is), so what a case can ask is what the *checker*
// believes. Both mis-declarations below quote `string` back — one at an
// element, one at the whole array — and an `array<mixed>` answer, which is the
// thing § 2 exists to avoid, could say it at neither position.
class Probe {
    public static function typed(): void {
        var $out = Core\Task::map([1, 2, 3], fn(int $n): string => "x");
        int $first = $out[0];
        array<int> $all = $out;
        echo $first, Core\Arr::count($all);
    }
}

Probe::typed();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
   --> case.nvs:17:22
%A
error[E0401]: expected `array<int>`, found `array<string>`
   --> case.nvs:18:27
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
